      "text": "Given that the arithmetic mean of $x$ and $y$ is 18, we have the equation:\n$$\\frac{x+y}{2}=18$$\nMultiplying both sides by 2 gives:\n$$x+y=36$$\nWe are also given that the geometric mean of $x$ and $y$ is $\\sqrt{92}$. The geometric mean is the square root of the product of $x$ and $y$:\n$$\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{92}$$\nSquaring both sides gives:\n$$xy=92$$\nNow we have a system of equations:\n$$\\begin{cases}\nx+y=36\\\\\nxy=92\n\\end{cases}$$\nWe can solve this system by considering $x$ and $y$ as the roots of a quadratic equation:\n$$(x-y)(x+y)=92$$\nSubstituting $x+y=36$ into the equation gives:\n$$36(x-y)=92$$\nDividing both sides by 36 gives:\n$$x-y=\\frac{92}{36}=\\frac{23}{9}$$\nNow we have a system of two equations:\n$$\\begin{cases}\nx+y=36\\\\\nx-y=\\frac{23}{9}\n\\end{cases}$$\nAdding the two equations gives:\n$$2x=36+\\frac{23}{9}=\\frac{333}{9}$$\nDividing both sides by 2 gives:\n$$x=\\frac{333}{18}=\\frac{111}{6}$$\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives:\n$$2y=36-\\frac{23}{9}=\\frac{297}{9}$$\nDividing both sides by 2 gives:\n$$y=\\frac{297}{18}=\\frac{99}{6}$$\nFinally, we can find $x^2+y^2$:\n$$x^2+y^2=\\left(\\frac{111}{6}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{99}{6}\\right)^2=\\frac{12321}{36}+\\frac{9801}{36}=\\frac{22122}{36}=\\boxed{614.5}$$\nThe answer is: 614.5",
